almost anything narrated by david  attenborough is always going to be  fascinating and inspiring however there  was one moment from the series dynasties  in particular that stuck with me in it  an alpha chimpanzee called david is  starting to lose his authority as he  gets older eventually he is overthrown  in a harrowing scene assaulted by a gang  of younger chimps that bite and strike  him and leave him for dead  even the camera crew find this upsetting  but like uh they cannot interfere  they continue to film him as he lies on  the ground  motionless  for days  [Music]  but amazingly david starts to move again  and slowly he regains his strength in  fact he regains his strength enough that  he's able to return to his group and  retake the throne by force  i'm not sure to what extent these  stories are fabricated but there is  clearly some truth here and what really  struck me is the way in which david was  able to recover his strength i can't  imagine a human being being left for  dead in the wilderness and recovering to  such an extent for many of us a twisted  ankle is a life sentence issues like bad  shoulders or knees can end up haunting  us for years or even decades  in the wild a limp like that would  severely hamper our ability to hunt for  food procreate and survive  here in the 21st century tennis elbow or  shoulder impingement can hamper our  training to the point that we lose all  gains and possibly even our enthusiasm  for training at all  moreover such issues can make regular  movement painful and difficult but  that's precisely the difference david  doesn't have the option to take it easy  he doesn't have any means to elevate his  injuries or apply ice he just had to get  on with it  if you have a bad knee so you completely  stop using your knee what message do you  think you're sending to your body  simple that you don't need to use that  knee and so it's not worth the resources  for your body to repair it  not only that but you will invite a  cascade of compensatory movement  patterns and imbalances that can lead to  further issues down the line  counter-intuitively the best way to heal  is actually to keep training but you  need to be smart about it  and at the same time this will help to  mitigate any loss of gains  in fact you might just find you improve  in ways you didn't even expect  so how do you train smart when injured  well not to brag but i get injured all  the time  mostly because filming the bioneer often  means pushing myself to get a shot even  when my body is saying stop also i fall  off stuff  speaking with my wife the last time she  was disinfecting my back like a scene  from lethal weapon i told her that i  learned an important lesson from this  latest fall  that i can heal from anything and i was  now ready to provide caution entirely to  the wind i will never die  that's not precisely what i think she  was hoping to hear  definitely be cautious people but there  is a strategy to continue training  safely through injury and i plan on  showing that here  let's start with what you don't do and  that is to move through pain if a  particular range of motion causes you  pain you should simply stop using that  range of motion pain is your body's  warning signal and if you don't heed it  you'll do more damage in the long run  if you imagine a muscle tear like  fraying on an elastic band it certainly  wouldn't be a good idea to then keep  stretching said band to the point of  further fraying  or even snapping  it's also important that you visit a  doctor or someone who knows what they're  talking about and can inspect you in  person  a doctor can advise on the cause of pain  and in some cases there might be  specific treatments that you need to  follow and couldn't have known about  but that's not to say that you shouldn't  move the affected area at all if you  have a regular training injury  in fact moving the affected area is the  best way to get blood flowing to said  area and to encourage healing  this increased blood flow will send  nutrients and building blocks that are  necessary for tissue repair this occurs  in the short term but also in the long  term that can even lead to an increase  in capillary to muscle ratio  that means permanently more blood supply  to that region and another way to think  about a muscle tear is as being similar  to the kind of muscle damage caused by  regular training but to the point of  being maladaptive if you have delayed  onset muscle soreness or doms from  regular training one of the best ways to  combat this is by training the same area  with light weights and high repetitions  the very next day  the same is true for some types of  injury as long as you move through a  range of motion that doesn't cause you  any additional pain  and this is why a lot of studies now  suggest that icing and elevating the  injury as has been recommended for the  longest time can actually slow down  recovery and even prevent full recovery  inflammation is not the enemy normal  inflammation is actually critical for  recovery  inflammation fights infections by  neutrophils and it kick-starts the  healing process by bringing fibroblasts  to produce collagen  and it increases growth hormone  production  in fact the use of anti-inflammatory  drugs may even inhibit hypertrophy  following a normal workout don't take  aspirin for those doms  and studies now suggest that icing  wounds specifically will not only slow  down healing but potentially even reduce  the total amount of recovery  this is still a controversial point and  there are some mixed studies but it  makes a lot of intuitive sense  inflammation is a natural response of  the body and so of course it has a  useful purpose it is not your enemy  walking in other forms of gentle cardio  can also be similarly beneficial by  encouraging blood flow around the entire  body  so the takeaway at this point is to try  training with very light weight or even  no weights at all and a smaller range of  motion that is pain free you can also  use slower movements  over time you should strengthen the  affected area and be able to gradually  increase the weight and return to normal  movement but take it slowly you can also  try blood flow restriction training that  is the correct application for this  particular protocol and it allows you to  increase blood in the target area even  more so while getting more hypertrophy  with lighter weights  studies show that this can indeed  accelerate recovery  either way don't completely stop all  movement  there are more things you can do during  your downtime too for injuries affecting  one limb it's important to continue  training the opposite limb this might  seem like a recipe for imbalance but  fortunately that's not the case thanks  to something called the cross-education  effect  simply this shows that training one limb  will promote strength gains in the  contralateral limb that's the opposite  limb even when that limb isn't being  trained directly this is due to the fact  that you're still training the skill and  building the neural pathways and these  can be referenced when moving the  opposite limb this won't necessarily  promote hypertrophy but you'll be able  to regain that loss size quicker by  maintaining some of your strength  of course you can also use this time to  focus on other areas of your training  for example there's no reason not to  continue training your upper body if you  have an injured leg you might though  find it's useful to use resistance  machines or bands and cables if you  struggle with picking up heavy weights  better yet train things that you  otherwise wouldn't have had the  opportunity to train or the inclination  for example you might benefit from  taking this opportunity to work on your  mobility or to train your grip strength  or to work on cardio we have a tendency  to do a lot of what we're already really  good at so this is the prime time to  work on those weak points  now rather than simply losing gains and  moving backwards you're just redirecting  your energy you're adding to your  arsenal  the best part is that thanks to  adaptations like increased my nuclei  better motor unit recruitment and  stronger tendons your weaker muscles  recover quickly and get to the same  point that are at much more quickly than  they did the first time  in other words once you recover you'll  be back to full strength but now that  added mobility and grip strength  that's how you turn a setback into a win  and for athletes whether you're a  martial artist a tracer a footballer you  might find you're unable to practice the  specific skills you need maybe you can't  jump or throw a run but you can still go  back to basics and work on some  foundational strength or even take the  time to train your brain and your visual  attention  with all that said i would also  recommend against training too  intensively in any capacity even if  you're still able to train hard while  avoiding damaged area it's still a good  idea to scale things back just a little  you really don't have to stop but it's  wise just to go a little easier on  yourself  the reason for this is simply that  recovery itself is something of a  stressor and is resource heavy if you  keep piling on more muscle damage and  challenge onto your body you'll need to  share those resources between those new  adaptations and the healing  and of course sleep well get lots of  sunlight and eat lots  so i hope you found this video useful  and interesting guys if you like this  sort of thing you may enjoy my ebook and  training program super functional  training there i take the same diverse  approach to training to build strength  but also resilience mobility speed  cognitive function and more there's a  link in the description  alternatively you could check out my  print book functional training and  beyond which is a introduction and  discussion around the whole subject of  functional training if you found this  useful then please leave a like and  share around what are your tips for  other people in a similar boat  either way thank you so much for  watching this one guys and i'll see you  soon  thanks for watching and  bye for now  you

